  "answer_text": "Great question! Here is a comprehensive overview of where opioids come from:\n\n---\n\n## What Are Opioids Derived From?\n\n### The Opium Poppy \u2014 The Original Source\n\nOpioids are a class of drugs derived from substances that occur naturally in the **opium poppy** (*Papaver somniferum*) and are widely used for pain relief and sedation. The opium poppy is an herbaceous plant native to Turkey but is now present in all temperate climate areas.\n\nCultivation of the opium poppy for its active constituents is thought to have begun about 3000 BCE, and opioids that occur naturally in the plant have been used specifically for relieving pain and for producing euphoria since at least the time of the ancient Greeks.\n\n---\n\n### The Three Categories of Opioids\n\nOpioids are broadly divided into three types based on their origin:\n\n#### 1. \ud83c\udf3f Natural Opioids\nNatural opioids are substances derived directly from the opium poppy, typically from the **resin of the plant's seed pod**. Natural opioids are also referred to as **opiates**.\n\nNatural opioids such as **codeine**, **heroin**, **morphine**, and **opium** can be extracted from the opium poppy.\n\n#### 2. \ud83e\uddea Semi-Synthetic Opioids\nSemi-synthetic opioids are made by **chemically processing natural opioids**, modifying them into new opioid compounds. While these drugs are man-made and don't occur in nature, natural opioids from the opium poppy serve as the \"raw material\" that manufacturers use to make them.\n\nHydrocodone (e.g., Vicodin), hydromorphone (e.g., Dilaudid), and oxycodone (e.g., OxyContin, Percocet) may be partially synthesized from chemical components of opium.\n\n#### 3. \u2697\ufe0f Synthetic Opioids\nSynthetic opioids are **entirely man-made**. Unlike semi-synthetic opioids derived from the opium poppy, synthetic opioids are made from other precursor chemicals that do not depend on the opium poppy at all. Synthetic opioid compounds are made to **mimic natural opioids** (as well as endorphins), allowing them to activate opioid receptors.\n\nSynthetic opioids include **fentanyl**, its analogues, and emerging non-fentanyl opioids.\n\nThe first fully synthetic opioid was **meperidine (Demerol)**, found serendipitously by German chemist Otto Eisleb at IG Farben in 1932. Meperidine was the first opioid to have a structure unrelated to morphine, but with opioid-like properties.\n\n---\n\n### How Do They Work?\nOpioids such as heroin and morphine exert their effects by **mimicking naturally occurring substances** called endogenous opioid peptides, or endorphins. Opioids work in the brain to produce a variety of effects, including pain relief.\n\n---\n\nIn summary, opioids are ultimately rooted in the opium poppy plant, whether they are directly extracted from it, chemically modified from its compounds, or synthetically designed to mimic its effects.",
